WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:05.778
Thirteen point eight billion years ago, our universe was unimaginably hot and dense.

2
00:00:06.128 --> 00:00:13.643
Everything that would ever exist, every star, every ocean, every heartbeat, was packed into something smaller than an atom.

3
00:00:13.993 --> 00:00:16.547
Then, space itself began expanding.

4
00:00:16.897 --> 00:00:23.188
In a fraction of a second, the universe grew exponentially—an extraordinary event called cosmic inflation.

5
00:00:23.658 --> 00:00:29.292
In the first millionth of a second, temperatures were so extreme that matter as we know it could not exist.

6
00:00:29.642 --> 00:00:32.288
But as the universe expanded, it cooled.

7
00:00:32.638 --> 00:00:37.613
Quarks combined to form protons and neutrons—the building blocks of atomic nuclei.

8
00:00:38.083 --> 00:00:43.993
Within the first three minutes, protons and neutrons fused into the nuclei of hydrogen and helium.

9
00:00:44.343 --> 00:00:46.042
The first elements were born.

10
00:00:46.392 --> 00:00:50.393
But it was still a blazing, opaque sea of particles and radiation.

11
00:00:50.743 --> 00:00:52.455
Light could not travel freely.

12
00:00:52.925 --> 00:00:58.677
Then, 380.000 years after the Big Bang, something extraordinary happened again.

13
00:00:59.027 --> 00:01:02.647
The universe cooled enough for electrons to join atomic nuclei.

14
00:01:02.997 --> 00:01:06.117
For the first time, stable neutral atoms formed.

15
00:01:06.467 --> 00:01:10.758
And suddenly… light could travel freely across the cosmos.

16
00:01:11.198 --> 00:01:13.634
That ancient light still surrounds us today.

17
00:01:13.984 --> 00:01:18.696
We call it the cosmic microwave background, the oldest radiation we can detect.

18
00:01:19.046 --> 00:01:23.114
The universe was now filled with hydrogen, helium, and darkness.

19
00:01:23.464 --> 00:01:25.584
The first stars had yet to shine.

20
00:01:26.973 --> 00:01:29.751
After the Big Bang, the universe went dark.

21
00:01:30.193 --> 00:01:33.366
For a hundred million years, there was nothing to see.

22
00:01:33.716 --> 00:01:37.704
Only cold clouds of hydrogen and helium, drifting through the black.

23
00:01:38.174 --> 00:01:39.860
But gravity was patient.

24
00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:42.422
Slowly, it pulled the gas inward.

25
00:01:42.772 --> 00:01:47.918
The clouds folded, collapsed and spun, growing denser and hotter at their hearts.

26
00:01:48.388 --> 00:01:51.179
Deep inside, the pressure became unbearable.

27
00:01:51.529 --> 00:01:54.426
The temperature climbed past ten million degrees.

28
00:01:54.776 --> 00:01:58.225
And then, hydrogen nuclei crashed together and fused.

29
00:01:58.575 --> 00:01:59.945
A star was born.

30
00:02:00.415 --> 00:02:01.245
Then another.

31
00:02:01.595 --> 00:02:02.426
And another.

32
00:02:02.776 --> 00:02:09.040
The first stars were giants, hundreds of times the mass of our Sun, blazing blue across the young cosmos.

33
00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:14.326
They burned fast and died young, exploding and scattering new elements into space.

34
00:02:14.796 --> 00:02:15.534
Carbon.

35
00:02:15.885 --> 00:02:16.728
Oxygen.

36
00:02:17.078 --> 00:02:17.764
Iron.

37
00:02:18.114 --> 00:02:21.208
The ingredients for planets, and one day for us.

38
00:02:21.558 --> 00:02:23.217
The cosmic dawn had begun.

39
00:02:24.967 --> 00:02:28.217
Around the newborn Sun swirls a disk of leftovers:

40
00:02:28.164 --> 00:02:31.480
gas, ice, and dust forged in dead stars.

41
00:02:33.139 --> 00:02:34.665
Dust sticks to dust.

42
00:02:35.326 --> 00:02:37.010
Pebbles become boulders.

43
00:02:37.668 --> 00:02:40.576
Boulders become mountains, miles wide.

44
00:02:42.247 --> 00:02:43.984
Then gravity takes over.

45
00:02:44.644 --> 00:02:48.460
The bigger they get, the harder they pull, and the faster they grow.

46
00:02:50.131 --> 00:02:52.539
Near the Sun, ice can't survive.

47
00:02:53.199 --> 00:02:55.949
So the inner planets are built from rock and metal.

48
00:02:57.607 --> 00:02:59.831
One world outgrows its neighbors.

49
00:03:00.491 --> 00:03:02.978
Every crash makes it bigger, and hotter.

50
00:03:04.651 --> 00:03:06.098
Hot enough to melt.

51
00:03:06.759 --> 00:03:09.364
Iron sinks to the center and becomes a core.

52
00:03:10.022 --> 00:03:12.101
Lighter rock rises to the top.

53
00:03:13.759 --> 00:03:15.022
No oceans.

54
00:03:15.683 --> 00:03:17.130
No breathable air.

55
00:03:17.788 --> 00:03:19.314
No solid ground.

56
00:03:19.972 --> 00:03:23.748
Just a glowing ocean of magma under a sky of ash and steam.

57
00:03:25.411 --> 00:03:30.032
This is Earth, 4.54 billion years ago.

58
00:03:29.795 --> 00:03:31.690
And its worst day is still coming.

59
00:03:35.867 --> 00:03:40.301
Earth is barely tens of millions of years old, and it's on a collision course.

60
00:03:41.925 --> 00:03:45.846
Another world, about the size of Mars, is closing in.

61
00:03:46.507 --> 00:03:47.783
We call it Theia.

62
00:03:49.399 --> 00:03:51.941
It doesn't hit head-on.

63
00:03:51.704 --> 00:03:55.086
It hits at an angle, at about ten kilometers per second.

64
00:03:56.705 --> 00:03:59.152
The blow melts much of both worlds.

65
00:03:59.813 --> 00:04:04.221
Theia's iron core sinks into Earth's and becomes part of our planet forever.

66
00:04:05.835 --> 00:04:10.743
Molten and vaporized rock blasts into orbit, wrapping Earth in a glowing ring.

67
00:04:12.373 --> 00:04:13.952
Some of it falls back.

68
00:04:14.612 --> 00:04:17.047
The rest pulls together into one body:

69
00:04:17.020 --> 00:04:17.876
the Moon.

70
00:04:19.487 --> 00:04:25.369
Born close, it looms huge in the sky, and an Earth day lasts only five or six hours.

71
00:04:26.985 --> 00:04:34.222
Ever since, the Moon has been drifting away, about four centimeters a year, roughly as fast as your fingernails grow.

72
00:04:35.851 --> 00:04:38.009
The collision nearly destroyed Earth.

73
00:04:38.669 --> 00:04:43.893
Instead, it gave us a steadier tilt, the tides, and the night's brightest light.

74
00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:49.957
Four billion years ago, Earth was hell.

75
00:04:50.307 --> 00:04:51.717
Almost no land.

76
00:04:52.067 --> 00:04:53.595
No oxygen to breathe.

77
00:04:53.945 --> 00:04:57.315
Just a black, restless ocean under a poisoned sky.

78
00:04:57.785 --> 00:05:00.852
But deep below, the sea floor was splitting open.

79
00:05:01.202 --> 00:05:07.506
Water plunged into the rock, met the planet's burning heart, and came screaming back up through volcanic vents.

80
00:05:07.976 --> 00:05:10.636
Some belched black clouds of iron and sulfur.

81
00:05:11.106 --> 00:05:15.621
Others raised tall white chimneys, warm and riddled with tiny stone chambers.

82
00:05:15.970 --> 00:05:18.485
And inside them, chemistry got busy.

83
00:05:18.835 --> 00:05:23.271
Simple molecules snapped together into amino acids, the bricks of life.

84
00:05:23.741 --> 00:05:25.716
But bricks alone don't build anything.

85
00:05:26.066 --> 00:05:27.555
They need instructions.

86
00:05:28.145 --> 00:05:32.449
Enter RNA, the first set of instructions for building with those bricks.

87
00:05:32.799 --> 00:05:35.590
A molecule that was both the blueprint and the builder.

88
00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:37.468
It could copy itself.

89
00:05:37.818 --> 00:05:40.451
For the first time, chemistry could remember.

90
00:05:41.041 --> 00:05:45.898
Wrapped in thin, oily bubbles, these copying chains became the first cells.

91
00:05:46.248 --> 00:05:47.697
Too small to see.

92
00:05:48.047 --> 00:05:49.601
Too simple to notice.

93
00:05:50.191 --> 00:05:51.153
But alive.

94
00:05:51.623 --> 00:05:52.533
Every tree.

95
00:05:52.883 --> 00:05:53.858
Every animal.

96
00:05:54.208 --> 00:05:55.341
Every one of us.

97
00:05:55.811 --> 00:05:59.615
It likely began here, in the dark, at the bottom of the sea.
